# Connection deadlines, shared by every TLS strategy and imported into the global
# options block of each Caddyfile.<strategy>. One copy, for the same reason as
# routes.caddyfile: a certificate source does not get to vary these.
#
# Caddy sets none of them by default -- a connection may take as long as it likes
# to send a request, and an idle one is kept for five minutes. That suits a
# public web server, not this: the only internet-facing listener, one TLS
# handshake per connection, and a legitimate client that makes a couple of
# requests a day. Without deadlines a few hundred sockets dribbling one header
# byte a minute hold the server for free, and since no request is ever made
# nothing downstream (the broker's in-flight cap, issuerd's) sees them -- and no
# token is needed to try it.
servers {
	timeouts {
		# Headers, from the first byte. A real client sends them in one segment;
		# ten seconds is beyond generous for a bad link and fatal to a dribbler.
		read_header 10s
		# Headers *and* body -- this is Go's ReadTimeout, not a second budget
		# after the first. The body is already capped at 16KB by routes.caddyfile,
		# so this bounds the rate at which those bytes are allowed to arrive.
		read_body 30s
		# Keep-alive. The helper builds a fresh agent per request and never reuses
		# a connection between them, so nothing legitimate is idle here at all;
		# this is a floor under that, not a budget anyone spends.
		idle 60s
	}
}

# `write` is deliberately absent. It is the deadline for producing the response,
# and the response comes from the broker: a slow directory plus a slow issuance is
# two 30s timeouts (KB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, twice) before the broker gives up on its
# own. Any write deadline short enough to matter against an attacker would cut
# off a legitimate slow ticket instead, and a client holding a connection open to
# *read* slowly costs the broker nothing that the in-flight cap has not already
# bounded.
